WHAT THIS WEB APP USES IT FOR
-----------------------------
Two things, both in backend/app/cdk_bridge.py:

1. 2D structure depiction with CIP stereo descriptors. CDK is the DEFAULT
   renderer for every picture the site shows; RDKit remains the fallback for
   processes with no JVM.
2. A second opinion on SMILES parsing. A string RDKit refuses is offered to
   CDK, whose valence and aromaticity models are more permissive.

HOW IT IS LOADED, AND WHY THAT MATTERS FOR THE LICENSE
-------------------------------------------------------
The jar is shipped unmodified and loaded at runtime as a separate program:
JPype hands it to a java.net.URLClassLoader inside the JVM the Orthonym engine
already runs. Orthonym (this web app) does not link against it, embed it, or
modify it. This is the same arrangement, and the same reasoning, that
backend/vendor/orthonym/NOTICE records for the OPSIN and centres jars.

The jar is an uber-jar: it bundles CDK's own dependencies. Their licenses travel
with them inside the archive.

REPLACING OR UPGRADING IT
-------------------------
backend/app/cdk_bridge.py resolves the newest cdk-*.jar in this directory, and
the ORTHONYM_CDK_JAR environment variable overrides the path entirely. Two things
must be re-checked after any version bump, because both depend on CDK internals
rather than on its public API:

  * cdk_bridge.self_check() must still pass. It fails if the CIP labelling path
    breaks -- which it can, silently, because centres-cli-1.5.jar carries its own
    partial copy of CDK and the two are resolved by URL ORDER, not by version.
  * backend/tests/test_cdk_bridge.py must still pass in full.
